          "statement": "<p>Define \\(f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}\\) by</p>\n\\[f(x)=\\frac{x^2}{1+x^2}.\\]\n<p>Prove directly from the \\(\\varepsilon\\)–\\(\\delta\\) definition that \\(f\\) is continuous at every \\(c\\in\\mathbb{R}\\). Do not use the algebra of continuous functions.</p>",
          "solution": "<p>Fix \\(c\\in\\mathbb{R}\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and set</p>\n\\[\\delta=\\min\\left\\{1,\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2|c|+1}\\right\\}&gt;0.\\]\n<p>If \\(|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\), then \\(|x+c|\\leq|x-c|+2|c|&lt;1+2|c|\\). Hence</p>\n\\[|f(x)-f(c)| =\\frac{|x^2-c^2|}{(1+x^2)(1+c^2)} \\leq(2|c|+1)|x-c| &lt;\\varepsilon.\\]\n<p>Thus \\(f\\) is continuous at every \\(c\\in\\mathbb{R}\\).</p>",
