          "number": 2,
          "title": "Lipschitz estimates on an interval",
          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let \\(S\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}\\). A function \\(f:S\\to\\mathbb{R}\\) is called <em>Lipschitz</em> on \\(S\\) if there exists a constant \\(K\\geq0\\) such that</p>\n\\[|f(x)-f(y)|\\leq K|x-y|\\qquad\\text{for all }x,y\\in S.\\]\n<p>Such a \\(K\\) is called a <em>Lipschitz constant</em> for \\(f\\).</p>\n<ol type=\"a\">\n<li>Prove that every Lipschitz function on \\(S\\) is continuous on \\(S\\).</li>\n<li>Prove that \\(f(x)=x^2\\) is Lipschitz on \\([0,1]\\), and give a Lipschitz constant.</li>\n<li>Prove that \\(g(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\) is not Lipschitz on \\([0,1]\\).</li>\n</ol>",
          "solution": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> Let \\(K\\geq0\\) be a Lipschitz constant, fix \\(c\\in S\\), and let \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). Set \\(\\delta=\\varepsilon/(K+1)&gt;0\\). For \\(x\\in S\\) with \\(|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\),</p>\n\\[|f(x)-f(c)|\\leq K|x-c|\\leq K\\delta =\\frac{K}{K+1}\\varepsilon&lt;\\varepsilon.\\]\n<p>Thus \\(f\\) is continuous on \\(S\\), including when \\(K=0\\).</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> For \\(x,y\\in[0,1]\\),</p>\n\\[|x^2-y^2|=|x-y||x+y|\\leq2|x-y|,\\]\n<p>so \\(K=2\\) is a Lipschitz constant.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> If \\(g\\) had a Lipschitz constant \\(K\\), taking \\(x=1/n^2\\) and \\(y=0\\) would give</p>\n\\[\\frac1n\\leq\\frac{K}{n^2},\\qquad\\text{hence }n\\leq K \\quad\\text{for every }n\\in\\mathbb{N},\\]\n<p>contradicting the Archimedean property.</p>",
