          "graded": true,
          "points": 10,
          "statement": "<p>Let \\(a&lt;b\\), and let \\(f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb{R}\\) be continuous. Suppose that \\(f(x)\\neq0\\) for every \\(x\\in[a,b]\\).</p>\n<ol type=\"a\">\n<li><p>Use the Extreme Value Theorem to prove that there exists \\(m&gt;0\\) such that</p>\n\\[|f(x)|\\geq m\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in[a,b].\\]\n<p>Deduce that \\(1/f\\) is bounded on \\([a,b]\\).</p></li>\n<li>Give an example of a continuous function \\(g:(0,1)\\to\\mathbb{R}\\) such that \\(g(x)\\neq0\\) for every \\(x\\in(0,1)\\), but \\(1/g\\) is unbounded. Justify your answer.</li>\n</ol>",
          "solution": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> The function \\(|f|\\) is continuous on \\([a,b]\\). By the Extreme Value Theorem, it attains its minimum at some \\(c\\in[a,b]\\). Set \\(m=|f(c)|\\). Since \\(f(c)\\neq0\\), we have \\(m&gt;0\\), and</p>\n\\[|f(x)|\\geq m,\\qquad \\left|\\frac1{f(x)}\\right|\\leq\\frac1m \\quad\\text{for every }x\\in[a,b].\\]\n<p>Thus \\(1/f\\) is bounded.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Take \\(g(x)=x\\) on \\((0,1)\\). This function is continuous and nowhere zero. Given any \\(B\\geq0\\), the point \\(x=1/(B+2)\\) lies in \\((0,1)\\) and satisfies</p>\n\\[\\left|\\frac1{g(x)}\\right|=B+2&gt;B.\\]\n<p>Hence \\(1/g\\) is unbounded.</p>",
