          "statement": "<p>Let \\(a&lt;b\\), and let \\(f:[a,b]\\to[a,b]\\) be continuous.</p>\n<ol type=\"a\">\n<li>Use the Intermediate Value Theorem to prove that there exists \\(c\\in[a,b]\\) such that \\(f(c)=c\\).</li>\n<li>Give an example of a continuous function \\(g:(0,1)\\to(0,1)\\) such that \\(g(x)\\neq x\\) for every \\(x\\in(0,1)\\). Justify your answer.</li>\n</ol>",
          "solution": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> Define \\(h(x)=f(x)-x\\). This function is continuous on \\([a,b]\\), and the range condition gives</p>\n\\[h(a)=f(a)-a\\geq0,\\qquad h(b)=f(b)-b\\leq0.\\]\n<p>If either endpoint value is zero, that endpoint is a fixed point. Otherwise \\(h(b)&lt;0&lt;h(a)\\), so the Intermediate Value Theorem gives \\(c\\in(a,b)\\) with \\(h(c)=0\\), or equivalently \\(f(c)=c\\).</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Take \\(g(x)=x/2\\). It is continuous, and for every \\(x\\in(0,1)\\) we have \\(0&lt;g(x)&lt;1\\) and \\(g(x)&lt;x\\). Thus it maps \\((0,1)\\) into itself and has no fixed point.</p>",
